# ABOUTME: Declares what `use Socket` installs: every exported name's :export/:export_ok
# ABOUTME: mark, tags and prototype. Measured on perl 5.42.0 with Socket 2.038.
#
# 88 of the constants are macros this platform does not define: each
# one's AUTOLOAD stub dies calling it (measured: "Your vendor has not
# defined Socket macro ..."), so each is None, never returning, as
# CORE.tpm types `die`. Four of those, the deprecated addrinfo constants
# AI_IDN_ALLOW_UNASSIGNED, AI_IDN_USE_STD3_ASCII_RULES,
# NI_IDN_ALLOW_UNASSIGNED and NI_IDN_USE_STD3_ASCII_RULES, have no
# prototype perl can report (measured: prototype() is undef for each)
# but die the same way, "The addrinfo constant ... is deprecated"
# (measured), so each is None too.
#
# CR, LF and CRLF are each both a () sub and a scalar of the same name,
# aliased to the sub's value (measured: `*CRLF = \CRLF()`); $CRLF is
# RFC 0001's example of a typed variable with :export_ok. All six carry
# the crlf tag along with all; bare `use Socket` installs none of them.
#
# Every other exported name with a () prototype is a constant, typed Int
# where perl returns a plain integer, or Str where it returns a packed
# byte string (measured: the four INADDR_* and two IN6ADDR_* constants).
#
# The rest -- pack/unpack/sockaddr_* for IPv4, IPv6 and Unix sockets,
# inet_aton/ntoa/pton/ntop, sockaddr_family, getaddrinfo and
# getnameinfo -- have no prototype perl can report and are typed by
# calling each with real arguments and observing what perl returns in
# both contexts (internal/infer/testdata/oracle/Socket.pl): several are
# declared `multi`, one candidate for list context (RFC 0001, "Context
# selects by return type") and one for scalar, since the same call
# returns a different shape in each -- unpack_sockaddr_in in list
# context is (port, addr); in scalar context, measured, it is the
# packed call's own return, a single Str. inet_aton, inet_ntop and
# inet_pton get a numeric address or a malformed one, never a hostname
# needing resolution; getaddrinfo and getnameinfo get numeric addresses
# and AI_NUMERICHOST/NI_NUMERICHOST, for the same reason.
package Socket;
sub AF_802 :export(all) () None;
sub AF_AAL :export(all) () None;
sub AF_APPLETALK :export(all) () Int;
sub AF_CCITT :export(all) () None;
